    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d636">

                <pb facs="0755" n="670" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg670"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">417.41/21</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the British Appointed Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grey</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-10-25"><hi rendition="#italic">October 25,
                        1919</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: Referring to Mr. Lindsay’s note No.
                    685 of September 20, 1919, and to my reply of October 8, 1919,<anchor n="15"
                        xml:id="d636-f1"/> relative to the so-called “Legation Claim” against the
                    Government of Nicaragua, <pb facs="0756" n="671" xml:id="pg671"/> I have the
                    honor to say that I have been informed that the Nicaraguan High Commission has
                    not as yet received notice from the British representative at Managua of the
                    acceptance of the Commission’s award in the matter of the Legation Claim. I beg
                    to suggest, therefore, that the appropriate authorities of the British
                    Government notify the High Commission of their acceptance of the award.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
